I. THE PROBLEM THIS INVENTION SOLVES
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Modern operating systems contain an architectural assumption
so pervasive it is rarely stated explicitly:

  Length values supplied by external sources — user space,
  network packets, device firmware, hardware interfaces,
  DMA engines, or inter-process communication — are treated
  as trustworthy inputs to memory and cryptographic operations.

This assumption is false. It has never been safe. And no
mitigation deployed in any major operating system as of the
filing date of this patent addresses it at the root.

The consequences of this assumption include:

  OUT-OF-BOUNDS READS
  A length field in a network packet, device response, or
  IPC message controls how many bytes are read from an
  internal buffer. If that field is not validated against
  the actual allocation, the read extends beyond the buffer.
  The Heartbleed vulnerability (CVE-2014-0160) is the most
  widely known example. It disclosed 64 kilobytes of process
  memory per request for two years before detection.

  OUT-OF-BOUNDS WRITES
  A length field controls how many bytes are written into a
  destination buffer. If the destination is smaller than the
  specified length, the write corrupts adjacent memory. This
  class includes stack overflows, heap overflows, and kernel
  object corruption. It represents the plurality of all
  privilege escalation vulnerabilities in the historical
  CVE database.

  CRYPTOGRAPHIC PARAMETER MISMATCHES
  AEAD construction functions — AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305,
  and similar — require that the ciphertext length provided
  to the decryption function exactly matches the length of
  the actual ciphertext, including the authentication tag.
  When the length parameter is derived from an untrusted
  source, the authentication check may be bypassed, truncated,
  or applied to the wrong region. The cryptographic guarantee
  of the construction is voided without raising an error.

  DMA BOUNDARY VIOLATIONS
  Direct Memory Access engines write to physical memory
  regions described by length and address parameters in
  descriptor rings. If those parameters originate from
  device firmware or untrusted configuration, the DMA engine
  can write to arbitrary physical memory, bypassing all
  virtual memory protections. IOMMU restrictions are
  insufficient when the length field itself is the attack
  vector.

  PRIVILEGE ESCALATION
  Kernel subsystems that process device driver responses,
  IOCTL parameters, or firmware-supplied buffers frequently
  use externally provided lengths to determine copy
  boundaries. A length value one byte beyond an allocation
  boundary can overwrite a function pointer, a security
  descriptor, or a capability mask in adjacent kernel memory.

The existing mitigation landscape does not address this:

  Stack canaries detect some sequential overwrites after
  they occur. They do not prevent the overwrite.

  ASLR randomizes addresses. It does not validate lengths.

  SELinux enforces access policy on objects. It does not
  inspect the length parameters of memory operations.

  TPM attestation verifies boot state. It has no visibility
  into runtime length values.

  IOMMU restricts DMA to mapped regions. It does not validate
  the length fields within DMA descriptors.

The OOB Guard invention addresses the root cause that all
of these mitigations leave untouched: the unvalidated length
value that reaches a memory or cryptographic operation from
an untrusted source.


