II. THE INVENTION — CORE ARCHITECTURE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The OOB Guard Module is a kernel-resident enforcement layer
that intercepts length values at the boundary between their
origin and their consumption.

The module operates on three primitives:

  PROVENANCE CLASSIFICATION
  Every length value in the system is assigned a provenance
  class at the point it enters a kernel-controlled path.
  The classification is not advisory. It is a type attribute
  that determines what operations may consume the value
  without further validation.

  BOUND DERIVATION
  For each operation that consumes a length, the module
  derives the maximum permissible bound from internal state
  — the allocation size, the protocol specification, the
  key schedule, or the hardware descriptor — independently
  of the externally supplied value. This derivation is
  performed once and stored in a protected region.

  ENFORCEMENT POINT
  Before any memory copy, cryptographic operation, or DMA
  transfer executes, the module compares the externally
  supplied length against the internally derived bound.
  If the supplied value exceeds the bound, the operation
  is rejected. The externally supplied value is never
  consumed. The bound itself is the only input to the
  operation.

These three primitives together constitute a complete
enforcement chain. No externally supplied length value
reaches a sensitive operation without passing through
all three stages.


