III. PROVENANCE CLASSIFICATION
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The invention defines four provenance classes. These are
not trust levels in the conventional security sense. They
are classifications of how a length value was produced
and what degree of internal verification has been applied.

  CLASS 0 — INTERNALLY DERIVED
  The length value was computed entirely from internal
  state by the kernel or a trusted module. No external
  input contributed to its value. Examples: the size of
  a kernel allocation returned by a verified allocator,
  the length of a key derived by a KBKDF from a hardware
  root key, the size of a descriptor computed from a
  verified firmware image. Class 0 lengths may be consumed
  directly by any operation.

  CLASS 1 — INTERNALLY VALIDATED
  The length value originated externally but has been
  validated against an internally derived bound and found
  within range. The validation has been recorded in the
  audit log with a timestamp and operation identifier.
  Class 1 lengths may be consumed by operations that
  accept validated external input, provided the specific
  operation was named in the validation record.

  CLASS 2 — CLAMPED
  The length value originated externally and has not been
  individually validated. The module has replaced it with
  the minimum of the supplied value and the internally
  derived bound. The clamped value is safe for memory
  operations. It is not safe for cryptographic operations
  where the length must exactly match a protocol-specified
  value, because clamping a cryptographic length silently
  truncates authentication coverage. Class 2 lengths are
  flagged in the audit log.

  CLASS 3 — UNTRUSTED
  The length value originated externally and has not been
  validated or clamped. Class 3 lengths may not be consumed
  by any memory, cryptographic, or I/O operation. Attempts
  to use a Class 3 length in a sensitive operation produce
  a violation event, reject the operation, and log a full
  audit record. Class 3 is the default classification for
  any length whose provenance cannot be established.

The classification system has a deliberate asymmetry: it
is always safe to classify a length at a lower trust level
than its actual origin warrants. It is never safe to
classify a length at a higher trust level than its origin
warrants. The module enforces this asymmetry by defaulting
to Class 3 for all unclassified lengths.


