IV. ENFORCEMENT DOMAINS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The OOB Guard Module enforces length provenance across
five distinct operational domains. Each domain has
properties that make naive enforcement insufficient and
require domain-specific handling.

DOMAIN 1 — MEMORY OPERATIONS

  Covered operations: memcpy, memmove, memset, bcopy,
  copy_from_user, copy_to_user, and their kernel-internal
  equivalents.

  The enforcement rule for memory operations permits
  Class 2 (clamped) lengths because the consequence of
  clamping is truncation of the copy, which is recoverable
  and auditable. The operation must still be logged when
  clamping occurs, because truncation of a copy may
  indicate a protocol violation or an attack.

  The specific bound used for memory operations is the
  lesser of: the size of the destination allocation, the
  size of the source allocation, and the protocol-specified
  maximum for this message type. All three values must be
  internally derived. The operation executes using the
  minimum of these three values, regardless of what
  external input supplied.

DOMAIN 2 — CRYPTOGRAPHIC OPERATIONS

  Covered operations: all AEAD encrypt and decrypt calls,
  HMAC computation and verification, key derivation
  functions, and digital signature operations.

  Cryptographic operations require Class 0 or Class 1
  lengths. Class 2 (clamped) lengths are rejected for
  cryptographic operations because clamping a ciphertext
  length silently removes bytes from the authentication
  tag, voiding the authentication guarantee without
  producing an error. A truncated AEAD authentication is
  cryptographically indistinguishable from no
  authentication at the application layer.

  For AEAD operations specifically, the module enforces
  a three-way consistency check:

    — The length of the plaintext or ciphertext buffer
      as reported by the allocator.
    — The length field in the cipher operation descriptor.
    — The length implied by the nonce, associated data,
      and protocol specification for this message type.

  All three must agree within the tolerance specified by
  the cipher construction. Disagreement among any two
  of the three values produces a violation event and
  the operation is rejected without execution.

DOMAIN 3 — DMA AND IOMMU OPERATIONS

  Covered operations: DMA descriptor submission, scatter-
  gather list construction, IOMMU mapping requests, and
  device ring buffer management.

  DMA operations require Class 0 lengths. The physical
  memory regions named in DMA descriptors must be derived
  entirely from internally managed allocation records.
  Length values from device firmware, PCIe configuration
  space, or ring buffer entries are Class 3 by default
  and must be explicitly promoted to Class 0 through
  a validation sequence that compares them against the
  kernel's internal allocation map before the descriptor
  is submitted to hardware.

  The IOMMU mapping for a DMA transfer is derived from
  the Class 0 bound, not from the device-supplied length.
  This ensures that even if a compromised device supplies
  an inflated length in its descriptor, the IOMMU mapping
  constrains the actual hardware transfer to the
  legitimate allocation.

DOMAIN 4 — DRIVER AND IOCTL INTERFACES

  Covered operations: ioctl parameter processing, driver
  read and write operations, character device interface
  handling, and kernel module parameter ingestion.

  All length values arriving through ioctl or driver
  interfaces are Class 3 by default. The driver is
  responsible for promoting them to Class 1 through
  the module's validation API before using them to
  control any memory or cryptographic operation.

  The validation API requires the driver to supply:
  the externally supplied length, the name of the
  operation for which it will be used, and the
  internally derived maximum bound for that operation.
  The module records all three in the audit log and
  returns the validated length if the supplied value
  is within bound, or rejects the operation if not.

DOMAIN 5 — NETWORK AND IPC PACKET PROCESSING

  Covered operations: network buffer processing, socket
  receive path, IPC message dispatch, and inter-VM
  communication channel ingestion.

  Length values in packet headers, message envelopes,
  and IPC descriptors are Class 3. The network stack
  must derive the actual permissible length from the
  socket receive buffer allocation and the protocol
  specification for the message type, then use that
  internally derived value as the bound against which
  the packet-supplied length is checked.

  For protocols with variable-length fields (TLS record
  layer, QUIC frame headers, SCTP chunk lengths), the
  module provides a protocol-aware validation function
  that incorporates the protocol's own length constraints
  as a secondary bound in addition to the allocation bound.


