V. AUDIT AND VIOLATION LOGGING
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Every interaction with the OOB Guard Module produces an
audit record. The audit log is append-only, stored in a
kernel-protected memory region, and periodically flushed
to a write-once storage backend. The log cannot be
modified by any process operating at privilege levels
below the OOB Guard Module itself.

Each audit record contains:

  — Monotonic timestamp at nanosecond resolution
  — Operation identifier (the specific function being
    guarded, as a string constant from the kernel image)
  — Provenance class of the length value
  — Supplied length (the externally presented value)
  — Bound (the internally derived maximum)
  — Outcome (PERMITTED / CLAMPED / REJECTED)
  — Calling context (kernel call stack hash, truncated
    to 64 bits, sufficient for correlation without full
    stack disclosure)
  — Verdict reason code

For REJECTED outcomes, the record also contains:

  — Full kernel call stack (protected, accessible only
    to audit consumers with appropriate clearance)
  — The domain (MEMORY / CRYPTO / DMA / DRIVER / NETWORK)
  — Whether the violation matches a known attack pattern
    from the module's violation signature database

The violation signature database is updateable through
a signed module interface. Signatures describe patterns
of repeated violations, cross-domain correlated violations
(a network packet length that, after rejection in the
network domain, appears in a subsequent IOCTL call with
identical characteristics), and temporal clustering that
suggests automated exploitation attempts rather than
software bugs.


