VI. THE CRYPTOGRAPHIC REPLACEMENT MAC ARCHITECTURE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The OOB Guard invention was developed in parallel with
the Cryptographic Replacement MAC (DSL-S) architecture,
also invented by Christopher T. Williams, filed under
USPTO 19/466,387.

These two inventions share a foundational principle:
trust in a communication or computation must be
established before the computation occurs, not inferred
from the absence of an error after it completes.

In the DSL-S architecture, the MAC layer does not trust
frame length fields from the network medium. It derives
the permissible frame length from the key schedule and
the authenticated session parameters, then validates
the received length against that internally derived bound
before the frame is processed. This is OOB Guard
enforcement applied at the MAC layer.

The DSL-S architecture operates in the 902-928 MHz ISM
band using Zadoff-Chu spreading sequences, BLAKE3 key
derivation, AES-GCM-SIV session encryption, and a
keyless ratchet cipher for forward secrecy. Each of
these components enforces length provenance at its
boundary. The BLAKE3 KDF produces key material of
exactly the length specified by the internal key
schedule, never by external request. The AES-GCM-SIV
nonce and ciphertext lengths are derived from the
session parameters, never from the ciphertext itself.

The OOB Guard Module and the DSL-S architecture together
demonstrate that length provenance enforcement is not a
patch applied to a broken system — it is an architectural
principle that, when applied consistently, eliminates the
entire class of vulnerabilities that depend on the
consumption of unvalidated external length values.


