VII. IMPLEMENTATION LINEAGE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The OOB Guard principle has been implemented across
multiple systems designed or documented by the inventor.
The following implementations are identified as prior
embodiments consistent with the claims of this patent.

  CTW SOCIAL PLATFORM (oob_guard.py)
  The CTW Social platform, deployed at advancedctresearch.com,
  contains a module (oob_guard.py) that constitutes the
  live reference implementation of Claims 1 through 6.

  The module defines explicit named bounds for every
  external input the platform processes:

    WS_MESSAGE:        65,536 bytes
    POST_CONTENT:         500 bytes
    DM_PLAINTEXT:      10,000 bytes
    DM_PASSPHRASE:        500 bytes
    DISPLAY_NAME:          50 bytes
    BIO:                  200 bytes
    SEARCH_QUERY:         256 bytes
    CHESS_FEN:            128 bytes
    CHESS_MOVE_UCI:         5 bytes
    ENGINE_RESP_BYTES:   4,096 bytes
    LINKIFY_INPUT:       2,000 bytes

  Every external input to the platform passes through
  a guard function before it reaches any processing
  path. Guard functions raise OOBViolation exceptions
  when the input exceeds its bound. Violations are
  logged with source identification and value context.
  A self-check routine runs at module initialization
  and verifies that the guard functions correctly
  reject values that exceed their bounds and correctly
  pass values that do not. The self-check is a
  precondition for platform startup.

  The client-side enforcement mirrors the server-side
  enforcement. The OOB object in the browser JavaScript
  applies the same named bounds to the same categories
  of input before any fetch, WebSocket send, or DOM
  insertion. The WebSocket receive handler rejects
  messages exceeding 65,536 bytes before parsing.
  The post submission handler rejects content exceeding
  500 bytes before the fetch. Neither the server nor
  the client trusts the other's length enforcement —
  both enforce independently at their own boundary.

  CTW-BSC CIPHER ENGINE
  The CTW-BSC zero-knowledge DM encryption system
  enforces length provenance at every cryptographic
  boundary. The PBKDF2 key derivation produces exactly
  68 bytes regardless of the passphrase length — the
  key length is internally specified, not externally
  requested. The AES-GCM-SIV nonce is exactly 12 bytes,
  generated from os.urandom(), not from any external
  parameter. The ciphertext length fed to the
  authentication verification is the length of the
  received blob minus the nonce length — an internally
  computed value, not the length field from the
  ciphertext envelope.

  The OOB guard on the ciphertext input (200,000 bytes
  maximum) ensures that even a maximally malformed
  ciphertext cannot consume unbounded server resources
  before the authentication check rejects it.

  GNSS EVIDENCE SCANNER
  The GNSS scanner enforces length provenance on all
  file inputs. The NMEA file upload is rejected before
  parsing if it exceeds 20 megabytes. The points.csv
  upload is rejected before parsing if it exceeds
  10 megabytes. These bounds are derived from the
  maximum plausible size of a legitimate GNSS session
  log, not from the upload itself.

  FS-5000 FORENSIC LOGGER
  The serial log JSONL/gz files produced by fs5000_dual.py
  enforce length provenance through GzipLog, which
  appends only complete records. The SSE bridge
  (log_bridge.py) reads the entire compressed file on
  each poll cycle and yields only records that
  successfully deserialize — malformed or truncated
  records are silently discarded rather than partially
  processed. The browser SSE consumer applies a
  BigInt conversion to the wall_ns timestamp field
  to prevent IEEE-754 precision loss from corrupting
  nanosecond timing — a length provenance enforcement
  applied to numeric precision rather than byte count.

  PSN (PACIFIED STRING NOTATION)
  PSN was developed by the inventor following the
  observation of a live RTLO Unicode exploit firing
  within a language model response during a security
  research session. The notation encodes dangerous
  strings as hex references in the format:

    PSN1:<type>:<hex>:<description>:<risk_class>

  This is length provenance enforcement applied to
  string encoding: the dangerous string never appears
  in executable form, only as a fixed-length hex
  representation with a declared risk classification.
  The consumer of a PSN record derives the actual
  string from the hex only after validating the
  risk_class against the consumer's own policy —
  an externally supplied string never reaches
  interpretation without passing through internal
  policy enforcement first.


