The following is a plain-language summary of the claims
as filed under USPTO 19/466,387. It is not a substitute
for the filed claims and is provided here for technical
orientation only.

  CLAIM 1 — LENGTH PROVENANCE CLASSIFICATION
  A computer-implemented method for classifying length
  values by provenance, comprising: receiving a length
  value and its origin designation; assigning a
  provenance class from a defined set of classes based
  on the origin; storing the class assignment in a
  protected kernel data structure; and making the class
  assignment available to enforcement functions before
  the length value is consumed by any memory,
  cryptographic, or I/O operation.

  CLAIM 2 — KERNEL-ENFORCED BOUND DERIVATION
  A system for deriving permissible length bounds
  independently of externally supplied values, comprising:
  a kernel module that computes the maximum permissible
  length for a named operation from internal allocation
  records, protocol specifications, and key schedules;
  stores the derived bound in a protected region; and
  supplies the bound to enforcement points rather than
  the externally supplied value.

  CLAIM 3 — CRYPTOGRAPHIC LENGTH CONSISTENCY
  A method for enforcing three-way length consistency
  in AEAD operations, comprising: computing the expected
  ciphertext length from the key schedule and protocol
  specification; comparing the expected length against
  the allocator-reported buffer length; comparing both
  against the length field in the cipher operation
  descriptor; and rejecting the operation if any two
  of the three values disagree beyond construction-
  specified tolerance.

  CLAIM 4 — DMA BOUNDARY ENFORCEMENT
  A system for constraining DMA transfers to internally
  validated memory regions, comprising: maintaining a
  kernel-managed map of valid DMA target allocations;
  deriving DMA descriptor length fields from this map
  rather than from device-supplied values; configuring
  IOMMU mappings from the internally derived lengths;
  and rejecting device-supplied descriptor lengths that
  exceed the mapped allocation bounds before descriptor
  submission to hardware.

  CLAIM 5 — AUDIT-GRADE VIOLATION LOGGING
  A method for producing tamper-evident records of
  length provenance enforcement decisions, comprising:
  writing to an append-only log for every enforcement
  decision; including in each record the supplied length,
  the derived bound, the provenance class, and the
  enforcement outcome; storing the log in a kernel-
  protected memory region; and flushing to write-once
  storage on a schedule that prevents retrospective
  modification.

  CLAIM 6 — SIGNED MODULE INTEGRATION
  A system for extending length provenance enforcement
  to third-party kernel modules and device drivers,
  comprising: a signed interface through which modules
  declare the bounds applicable to their external inputs;
  a validation function that modules invoke to promote
  external length values to Class 1 before use; and
  an enforcement mechanism that rejects any memory or
  cryptographic operation within a registered module
  that uses a Class 3 length value.


