IX. RELATIONSHIP TO PRIOR ART
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The OOB Guard invention is distinguished from prior art
on the following grounds.

  VERSUS BOUNDS CHECKING COMPILERS (SoftBound, CHERI)
  Compiler-based bounds checking instruments pointer
  arithmetic at compilation time and detects violations
  at runtime. It requires source code recompilation and
  does not operate on binary-only modules. The OOB Guard
  invention operates at the kernel system call and
  driver interface layer, requires no source access to
  the guarded subsystem, and enforces provenance
  classification rather than pointer arithmetic validity.
  The two approaches address different threat models and
  are complementary.

  VERSUS MEMORY TAGGING (ARM MTE, SPARC ADI)
  Memory tagging associates a tag with each memory
  allocation and verifies that pointer tags match
  allocation tags on access. It detects use-after-free
  and some heap overflow conditions. It does not classify
  length values by provenance and does not prevent
  an allocation-valid pointer from being used to copy
  an externally specified number of bytes that exceeds
  the intended operation size. The OOB Guard invention
  operates on the length parameter, not on the pointer.

  VERSUS ADDRESS SPACE LAYOUT RANDOMIZATION
  ASLR randomizes the base addresses of memory regions.
  It does not validate the length values used to copy
  data into those regions. An attacker who knows the
  length relationship between two adjacent allocations
  can overflow one into the other regardless of their
  addresses.

  VERSUS IOMMU ISOLATION
  IOMMU isolation restricts DMA operations to pre-
  mapped physical memory regions. When the attack vector
  is a length field within a DMA descriptor that causes
  the DMA engine to write beyond its intended region
  within the mapped range, IOMMU isolation provides
  no protection. The OOB Guard invention validates the
  length fields in DMA descriptors against internally
  derived bounds before the descriptor is submitted,
  preventing this class of attack within the mapped
  region.

  VERSUS INPUT VALIDATION FRAMEWORKS
  Application-layer input validation frameworks (JSON
  schema validators, protocol buffer length constraints,
  ORM field length limits) operate above the kernel
  and do not protect kernel operations. They are
  bypassable by direct system calls. The OOB Guard
  invention operates at the kernel level and cannot
  be bypassed by application-layer attack.


