X. OPERATIONAL GUIDANCE FOR IMPLEMENTERS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

This section provides guidance for engineers implementing
the OOB Guard principle in any language or environment.
It is not a specification — the specification is the
patent filing. It is a set of heuristics derived from
the inventor's experience implementing this principle
across multiple systems.

  DEFINE BOUNDS BEFORE READING INPUTS
  The bound for every external input must be defined
  in source code, as a named constant, before the
  input is read. If you cannot name the bound before
  you read the value, you do not yet understand the
  operation well enough to implement it safely.

  BOUNDS ARE NOT LIMITS — THEY ARE SPECIFICATIONS
  A bound is not a maximum that the system will tolerate.
  It is a specification of what the operation is designed
  to handle. Inputs that exceed the bound are not merely
  large — they are malformed with respect to the
  operation's specification. Reject them, do not truncate
  them silently.

  CRYPTOGRAPHIC LENGTHS ARE NOT CLAMPABLE
  If a length value controls how many bytes are
  authenticated by a MAC or AEAD construction, clamping
  it is not a safe fallback. A clamped authentication
  covers fewer bytes than the protocol specifies. The
  authentication may succeed on the clamped portion
  while the uncovered portion contains malicious content.
  Reject, do not clamp, for any length that controls
  the extent of a cryptographic operation.

  ENFORCE AT BOTH ENDS
  If a system has both a client and a server, both must
  enforce provenance independently. The server must not
  trust that the client has enforced its bounds. The
  client must not trust that the server will catch
  violations the client failed to prevent. Each boundary
  is an independent enforcement point.

  LOG EVERY VIOLATION — INCLUDING PERMITTED CLAMPING
  A clamped input is an anomaly. A rejected input is
  a potential attack. Both must be logged with enough
  context to reconstruct what happened. An audit log
  that records only rejections cannot detect an
  attacker who has learned to stay within bounds.

  SELF-TEST AT INITIALIZATION
  Every implementation of OOB Guard enforcement must
  include a self-test that runs before the system
  accepts any external input. The self-test must verify
  that values exceeding the bounds are rejected and
  that values within the bounds are accepted. A system
  that cannot verify its own enforcement before startup
  cannot be trusted to enforce anything after startup.


