XI. STATEMENT OF INVENTION AND PRIORITY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

This invention was conceived and reduced to practice by
Christopher T. Williams, operating as an independent
inventor and security researcher in Perris, California.

The invention was conceived from first principles
following the inventor's observation that all major
classes of memory safety vulnerability share a single
root cause: the consumption of an externally supplied
length value by an operation that did not independently
derive what that length should be.

The invention was reduced to practice through:

  — The oob_guard.py module deployed in production
    on the CTW Social platform

  — The client-side OOB enforcement object deployed
    in the CTW Social index.html

  — The CTW-BSC cipher engine, which applies length
    provenance enforcement to every cryptographic
    boundary in its implementation

  — The FS-5000 forensic logging system, which applies
    length provenance enforcement to serial, audio, and
    SSE data streams

  — The GNSS evidence scanner, which applies length
    provenance enforcement to all file upload paths

All implementations predate this filing and establish
the inventor's priority over the claimed methods.

